  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations for Office Administrative Services (NAICS 561110) stand at $300M in FY2026 to date, spread across 840 awards with an average award size of $357K. Obligations fell 54.6% year over year. The market has been steadily smaller since FY22 when obligations peaked at $915M, followed by $850M in FY23, $789M in FY24, and $659M in FY25, before the current $300M in FY26. Activity now is concentrated: the Department of Defense accounts for $158M of FY2026 to date obligations, while the next largest buyers are the Department of Homeland Security at $40.4M and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $30.7M. Vendor concentration is moderate: the largest contractor has $22.8M in obligations and the top two vendors each have about $22.7M to $22.8M. This fiscal year to date snapshot reflects lower overall obligation levels and a continued reliance on a handful of large buyers and prime vendors for most spending.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in FY2026 to date for NAICS 561110?",
        "body": "Obligations in FY2026 to date total $300M, down 54.6% year over year. The total comes from 840 awards with an average award size of $357K. Compared with prior years, FY26 is materially lower than FY25 at $659M and well below the FY22 peak of $915M. That drop shows fewer or smaller awards so far this fiscal year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate FY2026 to date obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $158M in obligations, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $40.4M and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $30.7M. Other notable buyers include the Department of Health and Human Services at $17M and the Department of Energy at $11.4M. The top vendors are KONIAG BRILLIENT, LLC at $22.8M and BOWHEAD MARINE SUPPORT SERVICES, LLC at $22.7M, with the third-ranked vendor at $17.8M."
      }
    ]
